{
  "id": "guided-search-theory",
  "term": "guided-search theory",
  "definition": "Guided-search theory is the idea that in visual search, attention is steered toward the most relevant or salient item before full identification occurs. This guidance reflects both goal-directed factors, such as what a person is looking for, and stimulus-driven factors, such as how much an item stands out, helping reduce the number of attentional shifts needed to find a target.",
  "field": "learning",
